{"meta":{"title":"G44 V simulated sound","version":2,"credit":"carpetshark","generator":"sim.py","units":"pascals at the listener; times in seconds from the trigger break"},"fs":48000,"c0":343.2,"fir_centre":20,"sources":{"meinke_pistol_ear":{"value":{"peak_db":157.5,"sd":0.3,"laeq8_db":78.1,"barrel_in":4.5,"mic_to_trigger_m":0.47,"mic_height_m":1.1},"status":"published","source":"Meinke, Murphy, Finan, Lankford, Flamme, Stewart, Soendergaard, Jerome 2014, Int J Audiol 53 S2 (CDC stacks 36705), table 4: a .22 LR semi-automatic pistol with a 4.5 in barrel, 40 gr high-velocity lead round nose, fired outdoors standing with two hands; 1/8 in microphone at the left ear position, 47 cm from the trigger, 110 cm above the ground; mean of 5 shots"},"meinke_rifles_ear":{"value":{"peak_db":[139.6,143.0,143.4,143.8],"barrel_in":[24,18,18,18.5],"laeq8_db":[63.8,65.6,66.5,66.3]},"status":"published","source":"same paper, table 2: four .22 rifles, same ammunition, same ear position"},"murphy_pistol":{"value":{"muzzle_mic_db":160,"ear_db":152,"instructor_db":135,"laeq8_muzzle_db":71,"laeq8_ear_db":63,"barrel_in":10,"mic_left_of_muzzle_m":0.6,"ear_mic_off_axis_m":0.35,"mic_height_m":1.63,"muzzle_height_m":1.53,"lw_hv_db":150,"lw_lv_db":145},"status":"published","source":"Murphy, Flamme, Campbell, Zechmann, Tasko, Lankford, Meinke, Finan, Stewart 2018, Int J Audiol 57 S1 S28-S41 (CDC stacks 211059), tables 2 to 4: a .22 LR semi-automatic pistol with a 10 in barrel, 40 gr rated 1280 ft/s, unsuppressed; pressure microphones 0.6 m left of the muzzle, 0.35 m either side of the firearm centre line at the ears, 1 m behind the head; LAeq8 over a 100 ms window; sound power by a 3 m hemisphere, 18 ms window"},"murphy_rifle":{"value":{"muzzle_mic_hv_db":152,"ear_hv_db":141,"muzzle_mic_lv_db":149,"ear_lv_db":140,"barrel_in":20.75},"status":"published","source":"same paper, table 2: a .22 LR bolt-action rifle, 1280 ft/s and 1050 ft/s ammunition. The barrel length (about 21 in) is the catalogue figure for that model, recalled, not from the paper"},"murphy_a_duration":{"value":0.0002,"status":"published","source":"same paper, note 1: all of the unsuppressed A-durations were less than 0.2 ms"},"murphy_suppressed_pistol_ear":{"value":{"peak_db":[128,133],"laeq8_db":[48,49]},"status":"published","source":"same paper: the suppressed pistol at the left and right ear. Everything that is not muzzle blast (action, port) is inside this, so it bounds the mechanical sound from above"},"flamme_bracket":{"value":[149.0,167.0],"status":"published","source":"Flamme, Wong, Liebe, Lynd 2009, Noise Health 11:231: peak levels 1 m left of 15 recreational firearms ranged from 149 to 167 dB (as summarised in the 2018 paper)"},"efflux_exponent":{"value":0.55,"status":"inferred","source":"peak overpressure ~ (muzzle energy efflux rate)^0.55: blast scaling length ~ sqrt(efflux), peak ~ length^1.1 (Fansler and Schmidt muzzle blast scaling; recalled, not re-read). Tested here against five published barrel lengths"},"decay_exponent":{"value":1.1,"status":"inferred","source":"peak ~ r^-1.1: weak-shock decay, same scaling"},"directivity_db":{"value":6.0,"status":"inferred","source":"level(theta) = level(90 deg) + 6 cos(theta) dB. Tested against the published muzzle-to-ear difference of the 10 in pistol"},"kinney_graham":{"value":"t+/W^(1/3) = 980 [1 + (Z/0.54)^10] / ([1 + (Z/0.02)^3] [1 + (Z/0.74)^6] sqrt(1 + (Z/6.9)^2)) ms; dp/p0 = 808 [1 + (Z/4.5)^2] / (sqrt(1 + (Z/0.048)^2) sqrt(1 + (Z/0.32)^2) sqrt(1 + (Z/1.35)^2))","status":"published","source":"Kinney and Graham 1985, Explosive Shocks in Air: positive phase and peak of a free-air burst of W kg TNT (4.184 MJ/kg) at scaled distance Z = r / W^(1/3). Recalled, not re-read. Used with W = the internal energy of the propellant gas at bullet exit, p V / (gamma - 1)"},"volume_source":{"value":"p(r, t) = rho0 / (4 pi r) d2V/dt2","status":"published","source":"far field of a compact volume source (Pierce, Acoustics, simple source). The gas leaving the muzzle displaces air at a rate that follows the energy efflux, so the pressure is positive while the flow builds, negative while it dies, and the two impulses cancel"},"jet_efficiency":{"value":0.005,"status":"inferred","source":"share of the mechanical power of a supersonic jet radiated as sound: 0.2 to 1 % (Lighthill 1954; NASA SP-8072 uses about 0.5 % for rocket exhausts; recalled). A 0.3 ms blow-down is not an established jet, so this is an upper estimate"},"jet_strouhal":{"value":0.2,"status":"published","source":"jet mixing noise peaks near f D_j / U_j = 0.2 (Tam 1995, Supersonic jet noise, Annu Rev Fluid Mech)"},"rise_note":{"value":"band-limited to 20 kHz","status":"numerical","source":"the shock is built at 16 x 48 kHz and low-passed; a 48 kHz system shows a lower peak than the 200 to 800 kHz systems of the published measurements (see check.json, sampled_peak_loss_db)"},"ground_sigma":{"value":200000.0,"status":"inferred","source":"flow resistivity of range ground, Pa s/m2: mown grass over soil is 150 to 300 kPa s/m2 (Embleton, Piercy, Daigle 1983). Impedance by Delany and Bazley 1970; spherical-wave reflection by Chien and Soroka 1975"},"ground_scatter":{"value":0.2,"status":"inferred","source":"share of the energy striking rough grassy ground that is scattered diffusely (Lambert law) rather than reflected like a mirror. Not measured"},"source_height":{"value":1.5,"status":"published","source":"muzzle 1.53 m above the ground for a standing shooter (Murphy 2018)"},"atmosphere":{"value":[20.0,50.0],"status":"stated","source":"20 C, 50 % relative humidity, 101.325 kPa; air absorption by ISO 9613-1"},"radiation_efficiency":{"value":0.00032,"status":"calibrated","source":"share of an impact's lost energy that leaves as sound. Set so the mechanical sound of the high-velocity load at the ear has the exposure by which the port-side ear exceeds the other ear on the published suppressed pistol (LAE 93.6 against 92.6 dB: 86.7 dB for the action alone, if the asymmetry is the action). That pistol has a different bolt, so this is an estimate"},"polymer":{"value":{"E":3000000000.0,"rho":1380.0,"nu":0.39,"eta":0.04},"status":"inferred","source":"frame and magazine: polyamide 6, conditioned (handbook 2 to 3.5 GPa, loss factor 0.02 to 0.06); density as in model/build.py"},"slide_material":{"value":{"E":20000000000.0,"rho":2600.0,"eta":0.03},"status":"inferred","source":"the slide is steel rails and breech in polymer; effective bending stiffness is a guess, density is the model's"},"steel":{"value":{"E":205000000000.0,"rho":7850.0,"eta":0.01},"status":"published","source":"handbook steel; the loss factor is for a part held in an assembly, inferred"},"hand_damping":{"value":2.0,"status":"inferred","source":"a gripped pistol: loss factors of the frame and magazine doubled"},"wall_mm":{"value":{"grip":2.5,"magazine":1.5,"slide":2.5},"status":"inferred","source":"wall thicknesses; not measured"},"contact_radius":{"value":0.005,"status":"inferred","source":"radius of the contact patches for the Hertz contact time"},"friction_efficiency":{"value":0.001,"status":"inferred","source":"share of the rail friction power (2 N x slide speed, action.json) radiated as sound"},"trigger_reset_time":{"value":0.13,"status":"inferred","source":"the shooter lets the trigger forward about 0.1 s after the shot; shooter cadence, not physics. action/ does not simulate the release"}},"structure":[{"body":"grip","f":1009.7,"eta":0.08},{"body":"grip","f":1860.6,"eta":0.08},{"body":"grip","f":3187.9,"eta":0.08},{"body":"frame","f":1188.8,"eta":0.08},{"body":"frame","f":3276.9,"eta":0.08},{"body":"frame","f":6424.1,"eta":0.08},{"body":"slide","f":2544.7,"eta":0.03},{"body":"slide","f":7014.6,"eta":0.03},{"body":"slide","f":13751.4,"eta":0.03},{"body":"barrel","f":1765.9,"eta":0.01},{"body":"barrel","f":11066.5,"eta":0.01},{"body":"magazine","f":1336.3,"eta":0.1},{"body":"magazine","f":1714.7,"eta":0.1},{"body":"magazine","f":4966.6,"eta":0.1},{"body":"spri